This uses a different idea from a stack. A segment tree stores the maximum value in every range. For each index, first check whether the range after it contains a value larger than `arr[i]`. If it does, binary search for the first position where that happens.

The circular part is handled by treating positions after `n - 1` as positions from the beginning again. The tree is built over two copies of the array.

```csharp
public class Solution
{
    private int[] tree;
    private int size;

    private int RangeMax(int node, int left, int right, int queryLeft, int queryRight)
    {
        if (queryRight < left || right < queryLeft)
        {
            return int.MinValue;
        }

        if (queryLeft <= left && right <= queryRight)
        {
            return tree[node];
        }

        int middle = (left + right) / 2;
        return Math.Max(
            RangeMax(node * 2, left, middle, queryLeft, queryRight),
            RangeMax(node * 2 + 1, middle + 1, right, queryLeft, queryRight)
        );
    }

    private void Build(int node, int left, int right, int[] values)
    {
        if (left == right)
        {
            tree[node] = values[left];
            return;
        }

        int middle = (left + right) / 2;
        Build(node * 2, left, middle, values);
        Build(node * 2 + 1, middle + 1, right, values);
        tree[node] = Math.Max(tree[node * 2], tree[node * 2 + 1]);
    }

    public List<int> NextGreaterElements(List<int> arr)
    {
        int n = arr.Count;
        List<int> answer = new List<int>(new int[n]);

        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++)
        {
            answer[i] = -1;
        }

        if (n == 0)
        {
            return answer;
        }

        int[] doubled = new int[2 * n];
        for (int i = 0; i < 2 * n; i++)
        {
            doubled[i] = arr[i % n];
        }

        size = 2 * n;
        tree = new int[4 * size];
        Build(1, 0, size - 1, doubled);

        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++)
        {
            int right = i + n - 1;
            if (RangeMax(1, 0, size - 1, i + 1, right) <= arr[i])
            {
                continue;
            }

            int low = i + 1;
            int high = right;
            while (low < high)
            {
                int middle = (low + high) / 2;
                if (RangeMax(1, 0, size - 1, i + 1, middle) > arr[i])
                {
                    high = middle;
                }
                else
                {
                    low = middle + 1;
                }
            }

            answer[i] = doubled[low];
        }

        return answer;
    }
}
```

Time complexity: `O(n log n)`. Building the tree takes `O(n)`, and each position uses range checks during a binary search.
Space complexity: `O(n)` for the doubled array, tree, and result list. The input list is not changed.

This pass starts from the end of a virtual array of length `2n`. The stack keeps possible greater values in decreasing order from the top's point of view. Before handling an element, smaller or equal values are removed because they cannot be the answer for it.

The first `n` positions are the real positions. The extra `n` positions only give the array its circular behavior.

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    vector<int> nextGreaterElements(vector<int> &arr) {
        int n = arr.size();
        vector<int> answer(n, -1);
        vector<int> stack;

        for (int i = 2 * n - 1; i >= 0; --i) {
            int value = arr[i % n];

            while (!stack.empty() && stack.back() <= value) {
                stack.pop_back();
            }

            if (i < n && !stack.empty()) {
                answer[i] = stack.back();
            }

            stack.push_back(value);
        }

        return answer;
    }
};
```

An edge case is an array with one value, such as `[7]`. There is no other position to compare with, so the returned result is `[-1]`. A descending array also correctly leaves `-1` for every position when no wraparound value is larger.

Time complexity: `O(n)` since each value enters and leaves the stack at most once across the doubled traversal.
Space complexity: `O(n)`. The input array is not modified.

A stack makes the repeated searching much faster. It stores indexes whose next greater value has not been found yet. While processing the doubled circular order, a larger value can answer several indexes at once.

Dry run for `[1, 2, 1]`: index `0` waits for a greater value, then `2` resolves it. The second `1` also gets resolved by the same `2` during the wrapped pass.

```java
class Solution {
    public int[] nextGreaterElements(int[] arr) {
        int n = arr.length;
        int[] answer = new int[n];
        java.util.Arrays.fill(answer, -1);
        java.util.ArrayDeque<Integer> stack = new java.util.ArrayDeque<>();

        for (int i = 0; i < 2 * n; i++) {
            int index = i % n;

            while (!stack.isEmpty() && arr[stack.peek()] < arr[index]) {
                answer[stack.pop()] = arr[index];
            }

            if (i < n) {
                stack.push(index);
            }
        }

        return answer;
    }
}
```

Time complexity: `O(n)`. Every index is pushed and removed at most once.
Space complexity: `O(n)` for the answer and stack. Equal values do not remove each other, which is important because the next value must be strictly greater. The input array remains unchanged.

Traverse the circular array twice from right to left, using i % n to simulate wrapping around.
Maintain a monotonic decreasing stack; pop all elements <= current because they cannot be the next greater element.
Stack top is the next greater element; if the stack is empty, answer is -1, then push the current element.

